## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the median under dynamic inserts and deletes is a common, practical data-structure task that tests understanding of balanced structures and handling edge cases with duplicates.

## Key Concepts
- Median of a dynamic multiset
- Heaps: max-heap for lower half, min-heap for upper half
- Lazy deletion via hashmap to avoid O(n) removal
- Balancing logic and handling duplicates with reference counting

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianSet {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();
    this.high = new MinHeap();
    this.delLow = new Map();
    this.delHigh = new Map();
  }

  _prune() {
    while (this.low.size() > 0 && this.delLow.has(this.low.peek())) {
      const count = this.delLow.get(this.low.peek()) - 1;
      if (count <= 0) this.delLow.delete(this.low.peek());
      else this.delLow.set(this.low.peek(), count);
      this.low.pop();
    }
    while (this.high.size() > 0 && this.delHigh.has(this.high.peek())) {
      const count = this.delHigh.get(this.high.peek()) - 1;
      if (count <= 0) this.delHigh.delete(this.high.peek());
      else this.delHigh.set(this.high.peek(), count);
      this.high.pop();
    }
  }

  _rebalance() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size() + 1) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  insertScore(x) {
    if (this.low.size() === 0 || x <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(x);
    } else {
      this.high.push(x);
    }
    this._rebalance();
  }

  removeScore(x) {
    if (this.low.size() > 0 && x <= this.low.peek()) {
      const count = this.delLow.get(x) || 0;
      this.delLow.set(x, count + 1);
    } else {
      const count = this.delHigh.get(x) || 0;
      this.delHigh.set(x, count + 1);
    }
    this._prune();
    this._rebalance();
  }

  getMedian() {
    this._prune();
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) return this.low.peek();
    if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) return this.high.peek();
    return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question simulates a live order book and tests dynamic range sums alongside top-k queries within a price band. It combines segment trees with per-node order statistics and requires careful handling of cancellations to maintain consistent state.

## Key Concepts
- **Dynamic segment tree** over the price domain for efficient range queries
- **Per-node multisets** to support kthLargest queries with duplicate prices
- **Order ID mapping** (idMap) for O(1) updates and cancellations
- **Amortized O(log² n)** operations: O(log n) tree traversal × O(log n) multiset operations

## Memory Handling
The idMap stores each active order's metadata, enabling O(1) lookups for updates and cancellations. When an order is cancelled, its entry is removed from both the idMap and the relevant multiset, ensuring no memory leaks. The segment tree nodes are created lazily on demand, only when prices are actually inserted.

## Cancellations
Cancellations require removing a specific quantity from a price node's multiset—O(log n) for the multiset operation plus O(log n) for updating the segment tree. This maintains accurate totals for both sum and kthLargest queries.

